\h Ezra
\toc1 Ezra
\toc2 Ezra
\toc3 Ezra
\mt1 Ezra
\is1 Introduction
\ip \bk The account of Ezra\bk*, is like a continuation of \bk The Chronicles\bk*—it tells us about the returning home of other Jews who had been taken to Babylon as captives, and the restoration of daily life in Yerushalem (Jerusalem) and of worshipping at the temple.
\ip It's divided into three main sections: 1. The declaration of King Koresh (Cyrus) of Persia (Heb. Paras) to allow a group of exiled Jews to return to Yerushalem from Babylon. 2. The rebuilding of the temple and its dedication, and the return to worshipping Yahweh there in Yerushalem. 3. The return of a different group of Jewish descendants to Yerushalem under the leadership of Ezra—the one knowledgable about God's instructions. He assisted the Israelis to return to their beliefs and customs, so that their worshipping of the true God wouldn't be forgotten.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 The first group of exiled Jews return home \ior 1:1–2:70\ior*
\io1 The rebuilding of the temple and its dedication \ior 3:1–6:22\ior*
\io1 Ezra leads other exiles to return to Yehudah (Judah) \ior 7:1–10:44\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Koresh returns some Jews
\rem /s1 Return of exiles to Jerusalem; Cyrus Helps the Exiles to Return; End of the Babylonian Captivity; Cyrus Commands the Jews to Return; Cyrus Allows the Exiles to Return
\p
\v 1 In¦217134 the first year¦217134 that Koresh¦217136 \add !(Cyrus¦217136)\add* was king¦217137 of¦217137 Persia¦217138 \add (Heb. Paras)\add*, Yahweh stirred¦217145 up¦217145 \add *his\add* spirit¦217149 in order to fulfil¦217139 what \add *he'd¦217142\add* spoken through \add the prophet\add* Yirmeyah¦217144 \add !(Jeremiah¦217144)\add*.\x + \xo 1:1: \xt Jer 25:11; 29:10.\x* \add ≈So¦217154\add* \add @the¦217154 king¦217154\add* distributed an \add official\add* written¦217162 notice throughout his¦217159 kingdom¦217159, saying¦217163,
\v 2 “Koresh¦217167, king¦217168 of¦217168 Persia¦217169, declares this: Yahweh¦217175, the¦217176 god¦217176 of¦217176 \add #heaven¦217177\add*, has¦217173 given¦217173 me¦217174 all¦217170 the¦217171 kingdoms¦217171 in the¦217172 world¦217172, and¦217178 he's appointed¦217180 me¦217181 to¦217182 build¦217182 a¦217185 residence for him¦217184 in¦217186 Yerushalem¦217186 in¦217188 Yehudah¦217188.\x + \xo 1:2: \xt Isa 44:28.\x*
\v 3 Anyone among¦217192 you¦217192 who's one of¦217195 his¦217195 followers is¦217196 free to go¦217199 to¦217200 Yerushalem¦217200 (in¦217202 Yehudah¦217202) \add to help\add* build¦217203 a temple¦217206 there for Yisrael's¦217209 god Yahweh¦217207—the¦217208 god¦217208 of¦217208 Yerushalem¦217213. May God go with you.
\v 4 Those¦217226 who live near these people¦217227 but¦217215 who¦217217 are¦217223 not going themselves, should¦217226 donate gold¦217230 and silver¦217229, and¦217231 goods and¦217232 livestock¦217232, plus cash as a voluntary offering¦217235 for¦217236 God's temple¦217236 in¦217239 Yerushalem¦217239.
\p
\v 5 Then¦217241 \add some\add* of the¦217243 leaders of¦217244 Benyamin¦217245 and Yehudah¦217244, and¦217246 \add some\add* priests¦217246 and¦217247 Levites¦217247—all¦217248 of whom¦217249 had had their \add #spirits¦217253\add* stirred¦217249 up¦217249 by God—left to¦217254 go¦217254 and build¦217255 Yahweh's¦217259 residence in¦217261 Yerushalem¦217261.
\v 6 Their neighbours had helped them by \add ≈donating\add* gold¦217271 and silver¦217270 utensils, goods and¦217273 livestock¦217273, and¦217274 expensive gifts¦217274, as well as the voluntary offerings¦217280 of¦217280 cash.
\p
\v 7 Then¦217283 King¦217283 Koresh¦217284 brought¦217285 out¦217285 the¦217288 equipment from Yahweh's¦217291 temple that¦217292 Nevukadnetstsar¦217294 \add !(Nebuchadnezzar¦217294)\add* had¦217293 brought¦217293 \add all the way\add* from¦217295 Yerushalem¦217295 and¦217296 had¦217296 put¦217296 in¦217297 the house¦217297 of¦217297 his¦217298 \add #gods¦217298\add*.
\v 8 \add *He\add* \add ≈put the treasurer¦217310 Mitedat in charge of bringing¦217300 them¦217311 out¦217311\add* and Sheshbatstsar, the¦217313 ruler¦217313 of¦217314 Yehudah¦217314, \add ≈in charge of accepting them and listing them\add*.
\v 9 There were¦217317: thirty¦217320 gold¦217319 basins¦217321, one¦217324 thousand¦217324 silver¦217323 basins, twenty-nine knives¦217325,
\v 10 thirty¦217332 gold¦217331 bowls¦217333, 410 silver¦217334 bowls, and¦217338 a thousand¦217341 other¦217340 utensils,
\v 11 \add ≈coming to a total¦217344 of 5,400 gold¦217347 and¦217348 silver¦217348 utensils\add*. Sheshbatstsar \add ≈took all¦217344 of¦217349 that with¦217356 the¦217358 group\add* of exiles¦217358 going from¦217359 Babylon¦217359 to¦217360 Yerushalem¦217360.
